---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Navbar from '../../components/Navbar.astro';
import wineries from '../../data/wineries.json';

const has = (frag: string) =>
  wineries.filter((w) => (w.varietals || []).some((v) => v.toLowerCase().includes(frag.toLowerCase())));

const cab = has('cabernet').sort((a, b) => (b.rating || 0) - (a.rating || 0)).slice(0, 8);
const syrah = has('syrah').sort((a, b) => (b.rating || 0) - (a.rating || 0)).slice(0, 8);
const grenache = has('grenache').sort((a, b) => (b.rating || 0) - (a.rating || 0)).slice(0, 6);
const temp = has('tempranillo').sort((a, b) => (b.rating || 0) - (a.rating || 0)).slice(0, 6);
const merlot = has('merlot').sort((a, b) => (b.rating || 0) - (a.rating || 0)).slice(0, 6);
const zin = has('zinfandel').sort((a, b) => (b.rating || 0) - (a.rating || 0)).slice(0, 4);
const petite = has('petite').sort((a, b) => (b.rating || 0) - (a.rating || 0)).slice(0, 4);

function listBlock(items: typeof wineries) {
  return items;
}
---

<Layout
  title="Temecula Red Varietals Guide | Best Reds to Taste"
  description="A guide to Temecula’s best red varietals — Cabernet, Syrah, Grenache, Tempranillo, Merlot, and more — with directory winery examples and tasting tips."
>
  <Navbar />
  <main class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-16">
    <p class="text-sm text-[var(--accent)] font-medium tracking-wide uppercase mb-3">Guide · Updated August 2026</p>
    <h1 class="text-5xl md:text-6xl font-serif tracking-tight mb-6">A Guide to Temecula’s Best Red Varietals</h1>
    <p class="text-xl text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-10">
      Temecula is a warm-climate Southern California wine country where ripe, fruit-forward reds and Mediterranean-leaning
      varieties thrive. This guide translates label names into a tasting plan — then points you to real estates in our
      <a class="text-[var(--accent)] underline" href="/wineries">Temecula wineries directory</a>.
    </p>

    <article class="prose prose-stone prose-lg max-w-none">
      <h2>What “Temecula red” usually means</h2>
      <p>
        Expect sunshine in the glass: darker fruit, softer edges than cool-climate reds, and styles built for patios and
        grilled food. That does not mean every bottle is simple. Many estates pour serious Syrah, structured Cabernet,
        and Spanish or Rhône blends that reward slow tasting. Your job is to match variety to mood — steak-night
        Cabernet is a different errand than a Grenache-friendly afternoon.
      </p>
      <p>
        Use this page with the
        <a href="/map">wineries map</a> so you are not driving across the valley between every Rhône pour. For a full
        itinerary wrapper, see
        <a href="/guides/weekend-in-temecula-wine-country">how to spend a weekend in Temecula wine country</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Cabernet Sauvignon</h2>
      <p>
        Cabernet is the comfort keyword for many first-timers. In Temecula it often shows ripe blackcurrant and plum with
        oak depending on the program. Ask whether you are tasting a straightforward estate Cab or a reserve/library pour
        with longer elevage. Pair with steak, short ribs, or aged cheddar — more ideas on our
        <a href="/pairings">pairings hub</a>.
      </p>
      <p><strong>Directory examples highlighting Cabernet:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        {listBlock(cab).map((w) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`/wineries/${w.slug}`}>{w.name}</a> — {(w.varietals || []).slice(0, 3).join(', ')}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <h2>Syrah</h2>
      <p>
        Syrah is a Temecula strength when you want pepper, dark fruit, and more savory depth than grocery-aisle sweetness.
        It sits at the center of many Rhône-inspired flights. If you like Northern Rhône vibes or dense California Syrah,
        build a day around hillside and specialist lists rather than only resort tasting menus.
      </p>
      <ul>
        {listBlock(syrah).map((w) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`/wineries/${w.slug}`}>{w.name}</a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <h2>Grenache</h2>
      <p>
        Grenache brings red fruit, spice, and often a more open-knit texture — excellent for afternoon tasting when heavy
        Cabernet would fatigue your palate. It also anchors GSM-style blends. Ask staff whether the pour is a bright
        patio Grenache or a darker, oak-kissed take.
      </p>
      <ul>
        {listBlock(grenache).map((w) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`/wineries/${w.slug}`}>{w.name}</a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <h2>Tempranillo</h2>
      <p>
        Tempranillo is a tell that an estate is playing in Spanish-leaning territory — a useful pivot if your group is
        bored of Cab-only flights. Expect cherry, leather-spice spectrum notes depending on oak and ripeness. De Portola
        and boutique lists are good hunting grounds; start with profiles that name Tempranillo up front.
      </p>
      <ul>
        {listBlock(temp).map((w) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`/wineries/${w.slug}`}>{w.name}</a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <h2>Merlot</h2>
      <p>
        Merlot still matters for plush texture and food flexibility. It is a bridge wine for mixed groups: softer tannin
        than young Cabernet, friendly with mushroom dishes and roast chicken. Do not sleep on it just because trend cycles
        moved on.
      </p>
      <ul>
        {listBlock(merlot).map((w) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`/wineries/${w.slug}`}>{w.name}</a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <h2>Zinfandel &amp; Petite Sirah</h2>
      <p>
        Where listed, Zinfandel can run jammy to peppery; Petite Sirah often brings inkier color and grip. Both can be
        high-alcohol patio wreckers if you stack them after two earlier flights — taste earlier in the day or share pours.
      </p>
      <ul>
        {[...zin, ...petite].map((w) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`/wineries/${w.slug}`}>{w.name}</a> — {(w.varietals || []).filter((v) => /zinfandel|petite/i.test(v)).join(', ') || (w.varietals || [])[0]}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <h2>How to structure a red-focused day</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Start lighter (Grenache or Tempranillo) before dense Cabernet or Petite Sirah.</li>
        <li>Limit to two estates if both pour full red flights; add a third only for a quick view stop.</li>
        <li>Eat protein-forward food midday — sugar-only snacks make reds taste harsher.</li>
        <li>Spit or dump when driving later; buy the bottle instead of “winning” the flight.</li>
        <li>Use <a href="/guides/best-temecula-wineries">best Temecula wineries</a> for overall shortlisting, then filter by varietal tags on each profile.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Beyond reds</h2>
      <p>
        Sparkling specialists and Chardonnay programs still belong in a balanced weekend. If your group splits between
        bubbles and bold reds, put sparkling earlier and reds after lunch. Thornton-style sparkling stops and Rhône
        hillside rooms can share a Saturday if you keep the drive honest — plot it on the
        <a href="/map">map</a> first.
      </p>
      <p>
        Independent guide on bellieacreswine.com. Varietal lists are planning cues from our directory data, not a promise
        of what pours every day. Confirm current tasting menus on official winery sites. 21+ for alcohol tastings.
      </p>
    </article>

    <div class="mt-14 grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 not-prose">
      <a href="/pairings" class="block p-6 rounded-3xl border border-stone-200 bg-white hover:shadow-lg transition">
        <div class="text-sm text-[var(--accent)] font-medium mb-1">Food</div>
        <div class="font-serif text-2xl">Wine pairing ideas →</div>
      </a>
      <a href="/guides/first-timer-tasting-route" class="block p-6 rounded-3xl border border-stone-200 bg-white hover:shadow-lg transition">
        <div class="text-sm text-stone-500 font-medium mb-1">Route</div>
        <div class="font-serif text-2xl">First-timer tasting route →</div>
      </a>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>
